# Line endings, decided here rather than by whoever cloned the repository.
#
# THE SHELL SCRIPTS ARE LF OR THE IMAGE DOES NOT BOOT. A CRLF entrypoint fails
# with `exec /app/entrypoint.sh: no such file or directory` — the kernel is
# reporting that it cannot find `/bin/sh\r`, which is the least helpful true
# statement it could make. This repository is developed on Windows, so the
# default would otherwise be CRLF and the failure would arrive at deploy time.
* text=auto
*.sh text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
docker/** text eol=lf

# A GOLDEN IS A BYTE FIXTURE, SO GIT CONVERTS NOTHING IN EITHER DIRECTION.
# test/golden.test.ts compares the whole document the renderer returns against
# the file, byte for byte and with no normalising, so `* text=auto` above would
# break it twice over. Once on the way out: these are written LF and a Windows
# checkout hands them back CRLF — `git ls-files --eol` already reports
# test/templates.test.ts as `w/crlf` in a tree where the file it tests is
# `w/lf`. And once on the way in: one golden deliberately holds a CRLF that
# arrived in a description from a Windows-authored CSV, because on a tearsheet
# `white-space: pre-line` makes it a line break in the deliverable — so
# `text eol=lf` would rewrite the fixture out of the index, which is worse than
# the problem. `-text` is neither conversion. See the header of golden.test.ts.
test/golden/*.html -text
